   "body": "**Adversarial review (Opus 5.5, cloud/review-1252-opus, round 1): REQUEST CHANGES** — head f446831\n\n1. **Medium**, `tooling/docs/check_legacy_pointers.py:23` (`HEADER`). When a header names two files, `\\((.+?)\\)` stretches across `) and <path2> (`, so the second file's items get checked against the first path. Probe: `// Port of …/AppSettingsView.swift (AppSettingsView) and …/AnswerView.swift (title, composerHeader `:765-819`) @ 87fe0d1c` fails with \"`composerHeader` is not in these lines\" against AppSettingsView. That pointer is correct, so CI goes red, against the issue's \"never fail on a pointer it cannot parse\". About 40 real headers use the `A (…) and B (…)` shape. None misfires today, but only by luck: ObjC selectors happen not to match. Fix: parse each `<path> (<items>)` pair on its own, or count as unchecked any items text that contains `) and ` / `) and of `. Add a test.\n2. **Medium**, `check_legacy_pointers.py:80-83`. The `lines a-b` rule picks the last word of whatever item comes before it, even prose. `(initOrResetMP:fromView:, the creation alert, lines 1-3)` checks `alert` and fails. Fix: attach the range only when the item before it ends in an identifier that looks like code (camelCase, PascalCase or backticked), and otherwise count it unchecked.\n3. **Low**, `check_legacy_pointers.py:139`. The fetch result is ignored. If the fetch fails or a sha is unreachable, everything is reported unchecked and the job stays green with no warning. Print a `::warning::` when the fetch fails or `checked == 0`. (`fetch-depth: 0` already brings in `legacy/duo-inner-cover`, so `--fetch` is redundant in CI, but harmless.)\n4. **Low**. `Symbol, :a-b` items (52 headers, e.g. `ReplyDraftPickerSheet, :1855-2001`) are not checked. The issue accepts this, but the README should list it, or the rule could attach the range to the last symbol, as it does for `lines`.\n5. **Detail**, `tooling/docs/README.md:14`. The README says \"leading `//` comment blocks\", but the code reads every block (`ImageUploadView.swift:212`). It also says \"anything that does not parse\" is counted, but a header the regex misses (`@ origin/master 21173e7`) is counted nowhere. A failure inside a block is reported at the block's first line.\n\n**Verified**: `python3 -m unittest discover scripts/tests` (207 OK). Checker `--fetch`: 24 checked, 403 unchecked, 0 wrong. Swapped the real pre-#1197 `AppIconSettingsView.swift` header back in: the checker flags 360-365 as wrong against the real `87fe0d1c` file. Confirmed `87fe0d1c:AnswerView.swift`: 765 = `private var composerHeader`, 819 = its closing brace, so 765-819 is correct. Ran the probes above. markdownlint: 0 errors. Read the CI job.\n\nRound 1 fixed in 6a63429: each `path (items)` pair of a header is checked against its own path (1); a `lines a-b` attaches only after a code-like identifier, and a plain word before a range is prose (2); a failed fetch or nothing checked prints a `::warning::` (3); a bare `:a-b` after a list stays unchecked and the README says so, since attaching it to the last symbol gave 8 false alarms on the repository's real headers, where the range may cover the whole group (4); README corrected (5); a `Port of` that does not parse is counted unchecked. Also corrected in `SeasonalDecorations.swift:4`: `setTheme` is declared at 363 (the snow block stays 382-388). Four new tests, 211 pass. Round 2 requested.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
